  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a single co-design experiment in central London, which may limit generalizability to other cultural or urban contexts.",
      "what": "A field experiment in central London engaged citizens in co-designing public space soundscapes using aural-visual scenarios, including acoustic-metamaterial and acoustic-wall interventions, yielding 144 design proposals. These proposals were evaluated using the Soundscape Perception Index (SPI) against 'Vibrant' and 'Calm' targets to measure the impact of group deliberation and structured expert input. The analysis revealed that collaborative group designs consistently outperformed individual ones, and that expert guidance specifically enhanced group performance for the challenging 'Calm' target.",
      "why": "The study demonstrates that expert technical input does not have to suppress citizen agency; instead, when integrated into a collaborative group process, it directly improves the acoustic performance of community-designed spaces."
